  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cube Roots and Perfect-Cube Structure",
    "description": "Understand cube roots and extract exact roots by grouping prime factors in threes.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Define ∛n as the number whose cube is n and contrast cube-root factor groups of three with square-root pairs. Teach prime factorization for exact integer cube roots, recognizing perfect cubes when every prime exponent is divisible by three, and estimating between consecutive cubes. Use examples such as ∛2744=14 and nearby integer cubes. Learners should group factors correctly, estimate a plausible root, and check by cubing."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cube roots reverse cubing. Prime factors must be grouped in threes, rather than pairs, to extract an integer cube root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "By definition, ∛a is the number whose cube equals a. Since 14³=2,744, ∛2,744=14. Use cubing to check a proposed integer root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
